1
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:25,960
[Wind Chimes Jingling ]

2
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:35,760
[ Man Speaking Arabic ]

3
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:47,840
[ Men Conversing In Arabic ]

4
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:55,440
Music [ Woman Singing
In Hungarian ]

5
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:30,640
Music [ Hungarian Song Ends ]

6
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:03,520
Music [ Woman Vocalizing ]

7
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:35,880
[ Shouting In German ]

8
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,840
[ Shouting ]

9
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:50,320
[ Engine Sputtering ]

10
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:07,960
[ Train Whistle Blows ]

11
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:21,400
- How are you?
- Okay.

12
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:23,480
Your leg
will be fine.

13
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:26,080
A lot of shrapnel came out.
I saved you the pieces.

14
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:29,200
You are the prettiest girl
I've ever seen.

15
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:30,960
I don't think so.
Here.

16
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:34,480
- Would you kiss me?
- No. I'll get you some tea.

17
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:38,240
- It would mean such a lot to me.
- Would it?

18
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:45,760
Thank you.

19
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:51,400
- I can't sleep. Would you kiss me?
- Oh.

20
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:54,160
- You're so pretty.
- Will you tuck me in, please?

21
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:56,920
[ Laughing ] Very funny.
Go to sleep now.

22
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:59,840
- Where's the doctor?
- Don't ask.

23
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:06,120
[ Conversing In Arabic ]

24
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:12,160
[ Conversation Continues ]

25
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:17,120
[ Gasping, Groaning ]

26
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:30,200
[ Breathing Heavily ]

27
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:38,280
[ Camel Grunts ]

28
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:46,400
[ Man Breathing Heavily ]

29
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:51,640
[ Fly Buzzing ]

30
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:55,200
[ Gasping, Gagging ]

31
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:06,960
[ Chattering ]

32
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:12,080
[ lndistinct Conversations ]

33
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:21,080
God, I hate giving blood.
Can't stand needles.

34
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:30,120
Is there anybody
from Picton?

35
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:33,120
Picton?
I don't know.

36
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:37,040
I'd like to see somebody
from home before I go.

37
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:47,200
- Do we have anybody from Picton?
- Why Picton?

38
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:49,920
He's from there. Edge of Lake Ontario,
right, soldier?

39
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:53,400
That's where your sweetie's from,
isn't it? Around there?

40
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:56,080
Ask him what company
he's with.

41
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:00,080
- Third Canadian Fusiliers.
- Third Canadian Fusiliers.

42
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:05,080
Does he know
Captain McGan?

43
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:09,440
He bought it yesterday.
Shot to bits.

44
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:11,960
- What did he say?
- Doesn't know him.

45
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:14,200
[ Shell Whistles, Explodes ]

46
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:16,400
Hana, get down!

47
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:20,640
[ Screaming, Shouting ]

48
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:23,760
[ Sobbing ]
He's dead. He's dead.

49
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:25,680
[ Shouting ]

50
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:28,760
[ Sobbing ]
He's dead.

51
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:30,680
He's dead.

52
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:33,680
[ Explosions ]

53
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:39,840
[ Bottles Clinking ]

54
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:49,000
[ Conversing In Arabic ]

55
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:01,440
[ Arabic ]

56
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:08,440
[ Breathing Heavily ]

57
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:11,200
[ Chanting ]

58
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:13,960
[ Continues ]

59
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:19,040
[ Chanting Continues,
Drumbeat ]

60
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:21,280
[ Arabic ]

61
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:24,720
[ Chanting Continues ]

62
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:27,120
[ Breathing Heavily ]

63
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:47,960
[ Chattering ]

64
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:54,480
[ Man ]
Name, rank, serial number?

65
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:56,480
[ Man #2 ]
No, sorry.

66
00:08:56,600 --> 00:08:59,160
I think I was
a pilot.

67
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:02,640
I was found in the wreckage of a plane
at the beginning of the war.

68
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:04,560
Can you remember
where you were born?

69
00:09:04,720 --> 00:09:07,200
Am I being interrogated?

70
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:10,880
You should be trying to
trick me, make me speak German.

71
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:13,200
- Which I can, by the way.
- Why?

72
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:15,920
- Are you German?
- No.

73
00:09:16,040 --> 00:09:19,640
How do you know you're not German
if you don't remember anything?

74
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:22,320
Might-- Might I have
a sip of water?

75
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:29,400
[ Sea Gulls Calling ]

76
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:31,560
[ Gulps ]

77
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:35,840
- Thank you.
- It's okay.

78
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:39,920
I remember
lots of things.

79
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:44,280
I remember her garden,
plunging down to the sea.

80
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:47,600
- Nothing between you and France.
- This was your garden?

81
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:50,480
- Or my wife's.
- You were married then?

82
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:54,720
I think so. Although I believe that
to be true of a number of Germans.

83
00:09:56,920 --> 00:09:58,720
Look.

84
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:02,360
[ Gasping ]
I have this much lung.

85
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:04,760
The rest of my organs
are packing up.

86
00:10:04,880 --> 00:10:08,040
What could it possibly matter
if I'm a Tutankhamen?

87
00:10:08,200 --> 00:10:11,920
I'm a bit of...
toast, my friend.

88
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:15,400
Sorry I can't help you.

89
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:19,640
[ Horn Honking ]

90
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:29,120
[ Breathing Heavily ]

91
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:31,280
Are you all right?

92
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:34,240
I know you hate to be moved.
I'm sorry.

93
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:40,640
- [ Honking ]
- Hana!

94
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:44,000
- Jan?
- Hana!

95
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:47,760
There's meant to be lace in the next
village. The boys are gonna take me.

96
00:10:47,920 --> 00:10:50,080
- You don't have any money, do ya?
- No.

97
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:51,840
- Just in case there's a sale.
- No.

98
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:53,440
Hana, I know you do.
Come on.

99
00:10:53,560 --> 00:10:55,040
- No!
- Oh, come on.

100
00:10:55,160 --> 00:10:57,520
I swear to God,
this'll be the last time. I swear.

101
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:00,040
She's a softie.
She loves me.

102
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:05,280
- I'll pay you back! I promise!
- [ Laughing ]

103
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:08,120
I'm not sewing
anything else for you.

104
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:10,320
I love you!

105
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:13,480
Whoa!
Pienza, boys!

106
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:20,000
[ Breathing Heavily ]

107
00:11:30,200 --> 00:11:32,000
[ Man ] Get some water
over here!

108
00:11:32,120 --> 00:11:36,000
Jan!
Oh, no!

109
00:11:37,480 --> 00:11:40,320
Hana, where're
you going?

110
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:43,520
- Stay back!
- Stop, Hana!

111
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:47,680
- Stay--
- [ Crying ]

112
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:53,120
No!

113
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:55,680
No!

114
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:41,080
- Hey! Hey!
- Don't move!

115
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:43,040
Don't move!
Don't move!

116
00:12:43,160 --> 00:12:45,280
Stand absolutely still!

117
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:47,960
You're walking in
a mine field!

118
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:52,920
Good.

119
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:55,680
That's good.

120
00:12:55,840 --> 00:12:58,760
Just stay still
for me...

121
00:12:58,920 --> 00:13:01,000
and we'll be fine.

122
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:04,920
What are you doing?

123
00:13:05,040 --> 00:13:08,000
What the bloody hell do
you think you're doing?

124
00:13:09,200 --> 00:13:11,920
[ Hana ]
It's Jan's.

125
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:15,680
[ Sighs ]

126
00:13:38,200 --> 00:13:41,120
[ Hana ] Are you in pain?
Do you need something?

127
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:43,040
- Yes.
- Okay.

128
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:47,280
Are you okay?

129
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:50,480
- You and Jan were--
- We keep moving him
in and out of the truck.

130
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:53,520
Why? He's dying.
What's the point?

131
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:56,080
We can hardly leave him.
Do you mean leave him?

132
00:13:56,240 --> 00:13:58,640
No, no,
I don't mean leave him.

133
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:10,080
This will help you.

134
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:14,200
I must be a curse.

135
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:17,640
Anybody who loves me...
[ Sighs ]

136
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:21,440
anybody who gets
close to me--

137
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:24,800
Oh, I must be cursed.

138
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:31,840
Which is it?

139
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:27,880
Music [ Single Piano Key Plays ]

140
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:37,920
[ Grunts ]

141
00:15:54,560 --> 00:15:58,240
- When he dies, I'll catch up.
- It's not safe here.

142
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:01,920
The whole country's crawling with
bandits and Germans and God knows what.

143
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:04,320
- It's madness! I can't allow it.
- The war is over.

144
00:16:04,480 --> 00:16:06,240
How can it be
desertion?

145
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:09,520
It's not over everywhere.
I didn't mean literally.

146
00:16:09,680 --> 00:16:12,480
This is normal, a shock,
for all of us, Hana.

147
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:15,840
I need morphine, a lot.
And a pistol.

148
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:23,520
If anything ever happened to you,
I'd never forgive myself.

149
00:16:26,520 --> 00:16:29,120
We're heading
for Leghorn.

150
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:31,360
"Livorno,"
the ltalians call it.

151
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:34,080
We'll expect you.

152
00:16:42,920 --> 00:16:44,680
Fine.

153
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:53,040
- Thank you.
- You're welcome, ma'am.

154
00:16:56,240 --> 00:16:58,960
- Oh, Hana.
- I'll be okay.

155
00:16:59,120 --> 00:17:01,920
I'll catch up.
Good.

156
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:12,080
[ Sighs ]

157
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:17,240
[ Muttering Song Lyrics ]

158
00:17:17,360 --> 00:17:20,000
[ Continues ]

159
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:34,840
[ Engines Droning ]

160
00:17:53,800 --> 00:17:55,720
[ Grunting ]

161
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:08,280
What-- What was
all that banging?

162
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:12,800
Are you-- Are you fighting rats,
or the entire German army?

163
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,040
No. I was repairing
the stairs.

164
00:18:18,160 --> 00:18:22,200
I found a library,
and the books were very useful.

165
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:25,480
Before you find too many
uses for those books,

166
00:18:25,640 --> 00:18:27,920
you might
read some to me.

167
00:18:28,080 --> 00:18:30,480
I think they're all in ltalian,
but I'll look, yes.

168
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:33,120
- What about your own book?
- My book?

169
00:18:33,240 --> 00:18:36,160
Oh, yes,
the Herodotus.

170
00:18:36,280 --> 00:18:39,080
Yes, you can read him.

171
00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:42,040
Oh. I found plums.

172
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:45,720
We have plums
in the orchard. There.

173
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:48,480
We have an orchard.

174
00:18:48,600 --> 00:18:52,480
Herodotus is
the father of history.

175
00:18:52,600 --> 00:18:55,160
- Do you know that?
- I don't know anything.

176
00:19:05,120 --> 00:19:06,880
Hmm.

177
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:16,800
It's a--

178
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:20,000
It's a very plum...

179
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:21,920
plum.

180
00:20:08,360 --> 00:20:09,760
[ Thud ]

181
00:20:11,400 --> 00:20:14,080
[ Man Speaking Arabic ]

182
00:20:14,240 --> 00:20:18,080
[ Responds In Arabic ]

183
00:20:39,280 --> 00:20:41,720
[ Arabic ]

184
00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:47,840
A mountain the shape
of a woman's back. Good, good.

185
00:21:11,040 --> 00:21:14,360
- Hello, Geoffrey!
- Madox, hello!

186
00:21:14,480 --> 00:21:17,720
- Welcome to the expedition.
- How do you do? Geoffrey Clifton.

187
00:21:17,840 --> 00:21:19,160
Splendid to finally
meet you all.

188
00:21:19,280 --> 00:21:21,080
This is Dante D'Agostino
and Diggy Bermann, our archaeologist.

189
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:23,440
- Beautiful plane.
- This is Sharif Al Fouad, Egyptologist.

190
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:26,360
- And this is my wife, Katharine.
- Hello.

191
00:21:26,480 --> 00:21:28,280
We're your
new apprentices.

192
00:21:28,400 --> 00:21:30,720
-Welcome to the lnternational Sand Club.
-[ Laughing ]

193
00:21:30,840 --> 00:21:34,400
- To the lnternational Sand Club.
- I'll get cups.

194
00:21:34,560 --> 00:21:36,800
- Marvellous plane. Did you look?
- Y es.

195
00:21:36,960 --> 00:21:39,840
Isn't it? A wedding present
from Katharine's parents.

196
00:21:39,960 --> 00:21:41,880
We're calling it
Rupert Bear.

197
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,240
- Hello. Geoffrey Clifton.
- Almasy.

198
00:21:44,400 --> 00:21:47,520
We can finally consign my old bird
to the scrap heap.

199
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:50,000
Mrs Clifton,
I'd like to present...

200
00:21:50,160 --> 00:21:52,880
- Count Almasy.
- Hello.

201
00:21:53,040 --> 00:21:55,680
Geoffrey gave me your monograph
and I was reading up in the desert.

202
00:21:55,800 --> 00:21:58,240
- Very impressive.
- Thank you.

203
00:21:58,360 --> 00:22:01,560
I wanted to meet the man who could write
a long paper with so few adjectives.

204
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:05,440
Well, a thing is still a thing,
no matter what you place in front of it.

205
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:09,280
Big car, slow car,
chauffeur-driven car.

206
00:22:09,440 --> 00:22:12,160
- [ Madox ] Broken car.
- It's still a car.

207
00:22:12,280 --> 00:22:15,360
- Not much use, though.
- [ Katharine ] Love?

208
00:22:15,520 --> 00:22:19,000
Romantic love,
plutonic love, filial love.

209
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:21,400
- Quite different things, surely.
- Uxoriousness.

210
00:22:21,520 --> 00:22:25,760
That's my favourite kind of love.
Excessive love of one's wife.

211
00:22:25,920 --> 00:22:29,520
- Now there you have me.
- [ Men Laughing ]

212
00:22:29,680 --> 00:22:32,080
- They're tourists.
- Absolute rot.

213
00:22:32,240 --> 00:22:34,520
They're highly recommended
by the Royal Geographic Society.

214
00:22:34,640 --> 00:22:36,480
She's charming and
has read everything.

215
00:22:36,640 --> 00:22:39,280
- He's meant to be a ruddy good flier.
- We don't need another pilot.

216
00:22:39,440 --> 00:22:41,280
He can make aerial maps
of the whole route.

217
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:43,960
You can't explore
from the air, Madox.

218
00:22:44,080 --> 00:22:46,800
If you could explore from the air,
life would be very simple.

219
00:22:47,920 --> 00:22:49,200
Contact.

220
00:22:51,320 --> 00:22:53,120
Contact.

221
00:24:06,800 --> 00:24:09,120
I should try
and move the bed.

222
00:24:09,240 --> 00:24:11,560
I want you to be able
to see the view.

223
00:24:11,720 --> 00:24:14,720
It's good. It's a view
from a monastery.

224
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:18,120
- I can already see.
- How?

225
00:24:18,280 --> 00:24:20,840
- How can you see anything?
- No, no, not the window.

226
00:24:20,960 --> 00:24:23,520
I can't bear
the light anyway.

227
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:25,920
I can see all the way
to the desert.

228
00:24:26,040 --> 00:24:27,840
I'm turning you.

229
00:24:27,960 --> 00:24:31,120
Exploring before the war,
making maps.

230
00:24:31,280 --> 00:24:34,240
Is there sand in my eyes?
Are you cleaning sand from my ears?

231
00:24:34,400 --> 00:24:37,760
No sand.
That's your morphine speaking.

232
00:24:44,760 --> 00:24:47,680
I can see my wife
in that view.

233
00:24:50,160 --> 00:24:52,960
Are you
remembering more?

234
00:24:53,080 --> 00:24:56,080
- Could I have a cigarette?
- Are you crazy?

235
00:24:57,480 --> 00:25:00,080
[ Coughing ]

236
00:25:00,240 --> 00:25:04,720
Why-- Why are you so determined
to keep me alive?

237
00:25:04,880 --> 00:25:08,120
[ Sighs ]
Because I'm a nurse.

238
00:25:33,040 --> 00:25:35,920
[ Sound Of Hana's Feet
Hopping ]

239
00:25:36,080 --> 00:25:38,880
[ Drumbeats, Chanting ]

240
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,400
[ Continues ]

241
00:25:47,600 --> 00:25:51,320
Music [ Arabic ]

242
00:25:55,040 --> 00:25:56,400
Bravo.

243
00:25:56,560 --> 00:25:58,960
[song] We have an
old-fashioned tomato [song]

244
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:01,280
[song] A Long lsland potato [song]

245
00:26:01,440 --> 00:26:04,400
[song] But yes
We have no bananas [song]

246
00:26:04,560 --> 00:26:06,640
[song] We have no bananas today Music

247
00:26:06,800 --> 00:26:10,320
Music [ Arabic ]

248
00:26:10,480 --> 00:26:13,720
Music [ German ]

249
00:26:13,840 --> 00:26:15,640
Music [ ltalian ]

250
00:26:15,760 --> 00:26:19,000
Music [ Arabic ]

251
00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:20,920
- Next!
- Katharine!

252
00:26:21,160 --> 00:26:23,840
[ Hana ] "The King insisted that
he would find some way...

253
00:26:23,960 --> 00:26:25,800
"to prove beyond dispute...

254
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:29,840
"that his wife
was fairest of all women.

255
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:33,400
"'l will hide you in your room
where we sleep,' said Candaules--

256
00:26:33,520 --> 00:26:35,960
- said Candaules."
- Candaules.

257
00:26:36,080 --> 00:26:38,520
"Candaules."

258
00:26:38,640 --> 00:26:40,640
Candaules.

259
00:26:40,800 --> 00:26:44,560
Candaules tells Gyges that the Queen has
the same practise every night.

260
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:47,480
She takes off
her clothes...

261
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:51,320
and puts them on the chair
by the door to her room.

262
00:26:51,440 --> 00:26:53,760
"'And from where you stand,

263
00:26:53,880 --> 00:26:57,200
you will be able to gaze
on her at your leisure.'"

264
00:26:58,640 --> 00:27:02,000
And that evening, it's exactly as
the King has told him.

265
00:27:02,160 --> 00:27:03,920
She goes
to the chair,

266
00:27:04,080 --> 00:27:06,040
removes her clothes
one by one,

267
00:27:06,160 --> 00:27:09,960
until she's standing naked
in full view of Gyges.

268
00:27:10,120 --> 00:27:14,520
And indeed, she was more lovely
than he could have imagined.

269
00:27:14,640 --> 00:27:18,040
But then,
the Queen looked up...

270
00:27:18,160 --> 00:27:22,080
and saw Gyges concealed
in the shadows.

271
00:27:22,200 --> 00:27:24,640
And although she said
nothing, she shuddered.

272
00:27:30,600 --> 00:27:32,640
And the next day,

273
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:36,320
she sends for, for Gyges
and challenged him.

274
00:27:36,440 --> 00:27:40,600
- And hearing his story,
this is what she said.
- Off with his head!

275
00:27:40,720 --> 00:27:42,400
[ Laughing ]

276
00:27:44,720 --> 00:27:47,600
She said, "Either you
must submit to death...

277
00:27:47,760 --> 00:27:50,480
"for gazing on that
which you should not,

278
00:27:50,640 --> 00:27:52,960
"or else kill my husband
who has shamed me...

279
00:27:54,840 --> 00:27:57,760
and become King
in his place."

280
00:27:59,920 --> 00:28:02,400
So Gyges kills the King,

281
00:28:02,520 --> 00:28:06,400
marries the Queen and becomes ruler
of Lydia for 28 years.

282
00:28:08,400 --> 00:28:10,160
The end.

283
00:28:12,760 --> 00:28:15,320
Shall I spin
the bottle?

284
00:28:15,440 --> 00:28:17,880
[ Madox ] So, Geoffrey,
let that be a lesson to you.

285
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,280
- [ Katharine ] D'Agostino!
- D'Agostino!

286
00:28:21,520 --> 00:28:23,760
Are you asleep?

287
00:28:24,880 --> 00:28:27,280
Yes.

288
00:28:27,440 --> 00:28:30,440
No. I'm dropping off.

289
00:29:09,440 --> 00:29:11,200
[ Cawing ]

290
00:29:11,360 --> 00:29:14,040
Go away!

291
00:29:14,160 --> 00:29:16,640
Go! Go!

292
00:29:26,240 --> 00:29:28,000
[ Grunts ]

293
00:29:30,840 --> 00:29:32,640
Buon giorno.

294
00:29:33,880 --> 00:29:36,640
- Hana?
- What do you want?

295
00:29:36,760 --> 00:29:39,520
I met your
friend Mary.

296
00:29:39,680 --> 00:29:42,720
She said I should stop and see
if you were all right.

297
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:47,360
Apparently we're neighbours. My house
is two blocks from yours in Montreal.

298
00:29:47,520 --> 00:29:50,080
Cabot,
north of Laurier.

299
00:29:50,160 --> 00:29:52,840
- Bonjour.
- Bonjour.

300
00:29:55,160 --> 00:29:57,040
For you.

301
00:29:57,200 --> 00:29:59,200
I'd like to take credit for it,
but it's from Mary.

302
00:29:59,320 --> 00:30:02,720
My name is David Caravaggio,
but nobody ever called me David.

303
00:30:02,880 --> 00:30:07,200
Caravaggio they find
too absurd to miss out on.

304
00:30:07,320 --> 00:30:08,880
Oh!

305
00:30:09,040 --> 00:30:11,320
[ Snickers ]
Oh!

306
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:13,520
Oh, shit.
Stupid hands.

307
00:30:13,680 --> 00:30:16,800
Let me do that.

308
00:30:22,440 --> 00:30:24,920
They're fresh.

309
00:30:25,040 --> 00:30:27,320
I haven't had
an egg in--

310
00:30:29,760 --> 00:30:32,120
Have you noticed
there are chickens?

311
00:30:32,240 --> 00:30:34,280
In ltaly, you get chickens
but no eggs.

312
00:30:34,440 --> 00:30:37,040
In Africa, they're always eggs
but never chickens.

313
00:30:37,200 --> 00:30:40,400
- Who's separating them?
- You were in Africa?

314
00:30:40,560 --> 00:30:42,240
Yes, I was.

315
00:30:42,400 --> 00:30:45,120
So was my patient.

316
00:30:45,280 --> 00:30:48,120
Look, I'd like to
stay for a while.

317
00:30:48,240 --> 00:30:50,040
I have to do
some work here.

318
00:30:50,160 --> 00:30:53,360
I speak the language.
There are partisans to be disarmed.

319
00:30:53,520 --> 00:30:57,080
We embrace them and see
if we can relieve them of their weapons,

320
00:30:57,200 --> 00:30:59,120
you know,
while we hide.

321
00:30:59,280 --> 00:31:02,600
I was a thief, so the army
thought I'd be good at it.

322
00:31:02,760 --> 00:31:05,360
- So you can shoot a pistol?
- No.

323
00:31:05,520 --> 00:31:08,160
Do you have a problem
with those?

324
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:11,320
No.

325
00:31:11,440 --> 00:31:13,920
- I should look at them before you go.
- Look.

326
00:31:14,040 --> 00:31:16,280
It's a big place.
We needn't disturb each other.

327
00:31:16,400 --> 00:31:18,520
I'll sleep
in the stable.

328
00:31:18,640 --> 00:31:20,560
It doesn't matter where I sleep.
I don't sleep.

329
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:23,600
I don't know what Mary told you
about me, but I don't need company.

330
00:31:23,760 --> 00:31:26,600
I don't need
to be looked after.

331
00:31:30,680 --> 00:31:33,720
There is a man
downstairs.

332
00:31:33,840 --> 00:31:37,040
He brought us eggs.
He might stay.

333
00:31:37,200 --> 00:31:39,840
Why?
Can he lay eggs?

334
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:41,920
He's Canadian.

335
00:31:42,080 --> 00:31:45,880
Why are people always so happy when they
collide with one from the same place?

336
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:48,720
What happened in Montreal
when you passed a man in the street?

337
00:31:48,880 --> 00:31:51,760
Did you invite him
to live with you?

338
00:31:53,200 --> 00:31:55,320
He needn't
disturb you.

339
00:31:55,440 --> 00:31:57,840
He can't.
I'm already disturbed.

340
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:00,600
[ Sighs ]

341
00:32:00,720 --> 00:32:04,400
There's a war. Where you come from
becomes important.

342
00:32:04,560 --> 00:32:07,880
[ Patient ]
Why? I hate that idea.

343
00:32:12,880 --> 00:32:15,680
Ah!
It's gonna work.

344
00:32:16,760 --> 00:32:19,200
Gently.

345
00:32:19,320 --> 00:32:21,960
[ Knocking ]

346
00:32:23,160 --> 00:32:24,960
Can I help?

347
00:32:25,080 --> 00:32:26,880
It's finished.

348
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:29,080
So, you're our
Canadian pickpocket?

349
00:32:29,200 --> 00:32:31,680
Thief I think
is more accurate.

350
00:32:31,840 --> 00:32:36,160
I understand you were in Africa.
Whereabouts?

351
00:32:36,320 --> 00:32:38,760
Oh, all over.

352
00:32:38,920 --> 00:32:41,960
All over?

353
00:32:42,120 --> 00:32:45,440
I kept trying to cover
a very modest portion and still failed.

354
00:32:45,560 --> 00:32:48,320
- Are you leaving us?
- Yes.

355
00:32:48,440 --> 00:32:50,840
Now's our opportunity
to swap war wounds.

356
00:32:50,960 --> 00:32:54,080
[ Laughs ]
Then I'm definitely going.

357
00:32:57,600 --> 00:33:00,240
Does she have
war wounds?

358
00:33:00,400 --> 00:33:02,400
I think anybody
she ever loves...

359
00:33:02,560 --> 00:33:04,720
tends to die on her.

360
00:33:04,880 --> 00:33:07,680
- Are you planning to be the exception?
- Me?

361
00:33:07,840 --> 00:33:09,920
[ Laughs ]
I think you've got...

362
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:12,240
the wrong end
of the stick, old boy.

363
00:33:12,400 --> 00:33:14,160
So...

364
00:33:15,120 --> 00:33:16,880
Caravaggio.

365
00:33:17,040 --> 00:33:20,080
Hana thinks
you invented your name.

366
00:33:20,240 --> 00:33:22,640
And you've
forgotten yours.

367
00:33:22,800 --> 00:33:27,640
I said that no one would ever invent
such a preposterous name.

368
00:33:27,760 --> 00:33:29,920
And I said you can
forget everything,

369
00:33:31,480 --> 00:33:34,240
but you never forget
your name.

370
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:36,640
Count Almasy.

371
00:33:36,760 --> 00:33:38,920
That name mean
anything to you?

372
00:33:39,080 --> 00:33:41,280
Or Katharine Clifton?

373
00:33:41,400 --> 00:33:44,160
[ People Chattering ]

374
00:33:49,600 --> 00:33:51,360
Oh, I'm sorry.

375
00:33:51,520 --> 00:33:54,480
- How much did you pay?
- Oh, hello.

376
00:33:54,640 --> 00:33:58,280
They don't see foreign women
in this market. How much did you pay?

377
00:33:58,440 --> 00:34:01,000
Um, 7, 8 pounds,
I suppose.

378
00:34:01,080 --> 00:34:03,360
- Which store?
- Why?

379
00:34:03,480 --> 00:34:06,640
You've been cheated.
Oh, don't worry, we'll take it back.

380
00:34:06,800 --> 00:34:08,560
I don't want
to take it back.

381
00:34:08,720 --> 00:34:11,720
- This is not worth 8 pounds.
- It is to me.

382
00:34:11,880 --> 00:34:14,200
- Did you bargain?
- I don't care to bargain.

383
00:34:14,320 --> 00:34:16,360
That insults them.

384
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:21,440
I don't believe that. I think
that you're insulted by me somehow.

385
00:34:21,560 --> 00:34:24,760
I'd be very happy to obtain
the correct price for this.

386
00:34:24,880 --> 00:34:27,360
I apologize
if I appear abrupt.

387
00:34:27,520 --> 00:34:29,760
I'm rusty at social graces.

388
00:34:34,120 --> 00:34:35,960
How do you find Cairo?

389
00:34:36,080 --> 00:34:39,680
- Did you visit the pyramids?
- Excuse me.

390
00:34:39,800 --> 00:34:42,360
Or the Sphinx?

391
00:34:45,480 --> 00:34:49,600
[ Almasy ] Latitude, 25, 33.
Longitude, 25, 16.

392
00:34:49,720 --> 00:34:53,440
We attempt to drive northeast of Kofer,
we'll leave our bones in the desert.

393
00:34:53,560 --> 00:34:55,840
- I disagree.
- You're Hungarian. You always disagree.

394
00:34:55,960 --> 00:34:57,880
- Good evening, gentlemen.
- Good evening.

395
00:34:58,040 --> 00:34:59,960
- How is your charming wife?
- Marvellous.

396
00:35:00,120 --> 00:35:02,240
She's in love
with the hotel plumbing.

397
00:35:02,360 --> 00:35:04,360
She's either in the swimming pool.
She swims for hours.

398
00:35:04,480 --> 00:35:07,200
She's a fish. It's quite incredible.
Or she's in the bath.

399
00:35:07,320 --> 00:35:10,280
- [ Laughing ]
- Actually, she's just outside.

400
00:35:11,720 --> 00:35:13,800
Chaps only
in the Long Bar.

401
00:35:18,120 --> 00:35:19,880
Mrs Clifton.

402
00:35:20,040 --> 00:35:24,280
You'll have to forgive us. We're not
accustomed to the company of women.

403
00:35:24,440 --> 00:35:27,880
Not at all. I was thoroughly
enjoying my book.

404
00:35:28,040 --> 00:35:30,720
- The team is in mourning, darling.
- Oh, really?

405
00:35:30,840 --> 00:35:33,960
I'm afraid we're not having much luck
obtaining funds for the expedition.

406
00:35:34,120 --> 00:35:35,880
Oh. Well, what'll you do?

407
00:35:36,040 --> 00:35:38,280
More modest expedition,
or even wait a year.

408
00:35:38,440 --> 00:35:41,240
- It's a disaster.
- Remind our families we still exist.

409
00:35:41,400 --> 00:35:43,240
Good heavens,
are you married, Madox?

410
00:35:43,360 --> 00:35:46,280
Very much so.
We all are.

411
00:35:46,400 --> 00:35:48,680
- Save my friend Almasy here.
- I feel much better.

412
00:35:48,800 --> 00:35:52,360
Don't you, darling?
We were feeling rather self-conscious.

413
00:35:52,480 --> 00:35:54,440
Let's toast, then.
Absent wives.

414
00:35:54,560 --> 00:35:57,120
- [ All ] Absent wives.
- And present ones.

415
00:35:57,240 --> 00:35:58,760
And future ones.

416
00:36:02,400 --> 00:36:05,160
Music [ Jazz ]

417
00:36:16,440 --> 00:36:18,920
Music [ Song Ends ]

418
00:36:19,040 --> 00:36:20,840
Thank you.

419
00:36:20,960 --> 00:36:23,560
Excuse me.
May l?

420
00:36:23,720 --> 00:36:26,440
Music [ Slow ]

421
00:36:37,720 --> 00:36:40,880
- Why did you follow me yesterday?
- I'm sorry. What?

422
00:36:41,040 --> 00:36:43,440
After the market,
you followed me to the hotel.

423
00:36:43,600 --> 00:36:47,480
I was concerned. A woman
in that part of Cairo, a European woman.

424
00:36:47,640 --> 00:36:49,960
- I felt obliged to.
- You felt obliged to?

425
00:36:50,080 --> 00:36:52,760
As the wife of
one of our party.

426
00:36:52,920 --> 00:36:55,760
So why follow me?
Escort me, by all means.

427
00:36:55,920 --> 00:36:58,960
But following me
is predatory, isn't it?

428
00:37:36,840 --> 00:37:39,920
[ Snoring ]

429
00:37:40,080 --> 00:37:42,920
Could I ask you
to move?

430
00:37:44,040 --> 00:37:46,000
I'm sorry,
but l--

431
00:37:47,800 --> 00:37:50,600
I'm sorry.
Of course.

432
00:37:50,720 --> 00:37:53,480
- It's just when you move.
- It was too rude of me.

433
00:37:53,600 --> 00:37:57,960
- I can't really bear the pressure--
- Are you all right?

434
00:37:58,080 --> 00:38:00,240
I was dreaming.
Awful.

435
00:38:36,360 --> 00:38:39,080
[ Crying ]

436
00:38:45,560 --> 00:38:48,880
[ Sobbing ]

437
00:38:49,040 --> 00:38:50,800
Hana.

438
00:38:53,480 --> 00:38:56,840
Hana, are you
all right?

439
00:38:57,000 --> 00:38:59,400
Leave me alone.

440
00:39:00,760 --> 00:39:02,560
[ Sobs ]

441
00:39:19,680 --> 00:39:21,760
You're in love with him,
aren't you?

442
00:39:21,880 --> 00:39:24,800
Your poor patient.

443
00:39:24,920 --> 00:39:27,680
You think he's a saint
because of the way he looks.

444
00:39:27,800 --> 00:39:30,720
I don't think he is.

445
00:39:30,840 --> 00:39:32,960
I'm not so
in love with him.

446
00:39:33,080 --> 00:39:37,160
I'm in love with ghosts.

447
00:39:37,320 --> 00:39:40,200
So is he.
He's in love with ghosts.

448
00:39:40,320 --> 00:39:42,440
What if I told you
he did this to me?

449
00:39:45,600 --> 00:39:48,680
How could he have?
When?

450
00:39:48,840 --> 00:39:51,240
I'm one of his ghosts,
and he wouldn't even know it.

451
00:39:51,360 --> 00:39:55,000
- I don't know what that means.
- Ask your saint who he is.

452
00:39:56,120 --> 00:39:58,200
Ask him
who he's killed.

453
00:40:01,480 --> 00:40:05,640
[ Sobbing ] Please, don't creep
around this house.

454
00:40:05,800 --> 00:40:08,000
See, I don't think
he's forgotten anything!

455
00:40:08,120 --> 00:40:10,840
I think he wants to forget!

456
00:40:12,760 --> 00:40:14,880
[ Clifton ]
Gentlemen, to mapmaking.

457
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:16,800
[ All ]
To mapmaking!

458
00:40:16,920 --> 00:40:19,120
And a special thank you
to Geoffrey and Katharine,

459
00:40:19,280 --> 00:40:23,240
without whose fund-raising heroics,
we'd still be kicking our heels.

460
00:40:23,360 --> 00:40:25,640
- To arm-twisting.
- [ All ] Arm-twisting.

461
00:40:25,760 --> 00:40:29,640
Did Katharine say Geoffrey
has to fly back to Cairo?

462
00:40:29,760 --> 00:40:32,360
Return the favour.
Take a few photographs for the army.

463
00:40:32,480 --> 00:40:34,720
Oh, um, what kind
of photographs?

464
00:40:34,840 --> 00:40:36,640
Portraits.

465
00:40:36,760 --> 00:40:39,080
The brigadier,
the brigadier's wife,

466
00:40:39,200 --> 00:40:43,000
the brigadier's dog, brigadier
by the pyramids, brigadier breathing.

467
00:40:43,160 --> 00:40:45,480
And I shall,
of course, be bereft.

468
00:40:45,600 --> 00:40:48,240
I'll finally be able to
explore the Cairo nightlife.

469
00:40:48,400 --> 00:40:51,120
I shall produce an authoritative guide
to the zinc bars and--

470
00:40:51,280 --> 00:40:53,840
I want to say hareems. Are we
in the right country for hareems?

471
00:40:53,960 --> 00:40:55,720
[ Madox ]
Harems.

472
00:41:00,760 --> 00:41:03,120
- Bye, my love.
- Travel safe.

473
00:41:03,280 --> 00:41:05,360
Catch up
in a week.

474
00:41:07,960 --> 00:41:11,080
Clifton,
safe journey.

475
00:41:11,200 --> 00:41:15,240
You too. Good luck.
Glad the funds have turned up.

476
00:41:15,360 --> 00:41:18,120
Clifton.

477
00:41:21,240 --> 00:41:23,640
This is probably
none of my business.

478
00:41:23,800 --> 00:41:26,440
Your wife.

479
00:41:26,560 --> 00:41:29,640
- Think it's appropriate to leave her?
- Appropriate?

480
00:41:29,800 --> 00:41:33,320
Well, the desert is, it's, uh--
for a woman, it's very tough.

481
00:41:33,480 --> 00:41:35,560
I wonder if it's
not too much for her.

482
00:41:35,720 --> 00:41:38,040
Are you mad? Katharine loves it here.
She told me yesterday.

483
00:41:38,200 --> 00:41:41,000
- All the same, were I you--
- I've known Katharine
since she was three.

484
00:41:41,160 --> 00:41:43,720
We were practically brother and sister
before we were man and wife.

485
00:41:43,800 --> 00:41:46,080
I think I'd know what is and what isn't
too much for her.

486
00:41:46,200 --> 00:41:48,840
I think
she'd know herself.

487
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:50,760
Very well.

488
00:41:50,920 --> 00:41:53,640
Why are you people
so threatened by a woman?

489
00:42:04,080 --> 00:42:06,440
Music [ Piano ]

490
00:42:30,040 --> 00:42:31,840
[ Tapping ]

491
00:42:34,640 --> 00:42:36,680
I have come to love...

492
00:42:36,800 --> 00:42:40,360
that little tap of the
fingernail against the syringe.

493
00:42:41,480 --> 00:42:44,040
Tap.

494
00:42:44,160 --> 00:42:46,840
Tap.

495
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:48,760
Tap.

496
00:42:48,920 --> 00:42:52,360
Music [ Continues ]

497
00:43:08,720 --> 00:43:12,120
- [ Gunshot ]
- Stop playing! Please, stop playing.

498
00:43:16,680 --> 00:43:19,400
I don't have the key
to that door. You--

499
00:43:22,320 --> 00:43:25,360
The Germans were here.
The Germans were all over this area.

500
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:27,800
They left mines
everywhere.

501
00:43:27,960 --> 00:43:30,280
The pianos were their
favourite hiding places.

502
00:43:30,400 --> 00:43:32,680
I see.
Sorry.

503
00:43:37,840 --> 00:43:41,320
Then maybe you're safe
as long as you only play Bach.

504
00:43:42,840 --> 00:43:44,880
He's German.

505
00:43:46,080 --> 00:43:48,640
Is something funny?

506
00:43:48,760 --> 00:43:50,960
No. I'm sorry.

507
00:44:05,080 --> 00:44:07,200
I've met you before.

508
00:44:07,320 --> 00:44:10,600
I don't think so.

509
00:44:20,560 --> 00:44:22,360
Look. See?

510
00:44:22,520 --> 00:44:25,320
Move that,
and no more Bach.

511
00:44:25,480 --> 00:44:28,680
Lieutenant!
Are you all right, Sir?

512
00:44:28,800 --> 00:44:31,080
Fine, Sergeant.

513
00:44:31,240 --> 00:44:33,000
So you're working
with the ltalians.

514
00:44:33,160 --> 00:44:35,080
Yeah, trying to get them
to give up their weapons.

515
00:44:35,240 --> 00:44:37,800
I was a thief, so the army
thought I'd be good at it.

516
00:44:37,880 --> 00:44:40,120
I like the ltalians.

517
00:44:40,280 --> 00:44:42,400
[ Hana ]
He wants us to move out.

518
00:44:42,520 --> 00:44:45,800
He says there could be
50 more mines in the building.

519
00:44:45,920 --> 00:44:50,080
[ Hana ] He thinks I'm mad
because I laugh at him.

520
00:44:50,200 --> 00:44:53,000
He's lndian.

521
00:44:53,120 --> 00:44:56,760
- He wears a turban.
- No, he's Sikh.

522
00:44:56,920 --> 00:44:59,160
If he wears a turban,
he's Sikh.

523
00:44:59,320 --> 00:45:01,160
I'll probably marry him.

524
00:45:01,280 --> 00:45:04,320
Really?
That's sudden.

525
00:45:04,440 --> 00:45:06,280
My mother
always told me...

526
00:45:06,440 --> 00:45:09,960
I would summon my husband
by playing the piano.

527
00:45:11,440 --> 00:45:14,160
[ Breathing Heavily ]

528
00:45:15,960 --> 00:45:19,680
- I liked it better when
there were just the two of us.
- Why? Is he staying?

529
00:45:19,800 --> 00:45:22,360
With his sergeant,
a Mr Hardy.

530
00:45:23,760 --> 00:45:27,480
We should charge.
Doesn't anyone have a job to do?

531
00:45:27,640 --> 00:45:31,320
They have to clear all the roads
of mines. That's a big job.

532
00:45:31,480 --> 00:45:34,440
In that case, l--
I suppose we can't charge.

533
00:45:34,600 --> 00:45:36,920
No, we can't.

534
00:45:39,760 --> 00:45:42,000
Thank you.

535
00:45:45,640 --> 00:45:48,360
[ Engines Drone ]

536
00:46:07,360 --> 00:46:11,680
[ Muttering Song Lyrics ]

537
00:46:20,240 --> 00:46:22,480
[song] Flat foot floogee
with the floy floy [song]

538
00:46:22,640 --> 00:46:27,120
Music [ Humming ]

539
00:46:27,280 --> 00:46:29,840
[song] Flat foot floogee
with the floy floy [song]

540
00:46:29,920 --> 00:46:32,280
[song] Flat foot floogee
with the floy floy [song]

541
00:46:32,440 --> 00:46:36,520
I've been thinking. How does someone
like you decide to come to the desert?

542
00:46:36,640 --> 00:46:38,440
What is it? You--

543
00:46:38,560 --> 00:46:41,640
You're doing whatever you're doing in
your castle, or wherever it is you live,

544
00:46:41,760 --> 00:46:45,160
and one day you say,
"l have to get to the desert," or what?

545
00:46:45,320 --> 00:46:49,800
I once travelled with a guide
who was taking me to Faya.

546
00:46:49,960 --> 00:46:53,600
He didn't speak
for nine hours.

547
00:46:56,080 --> 00:47:00,480
At the end of it, he pointed
at the horizon and said, "Faya."

548
00:47:00,600 --> 00:47:03,400
That was a good day.

549
00:47:13,680 --> 00:47:16,040
Actually, you sing.

550
00:47:17,880 --> 00:47:20,200
- What?
- You sing.

551
00:47:20,360 --> 00:47:22,640
All the time.

552
00:47:22,800 --> 00:47:24,720
- I do not.
- Ask Al Auf.

553
00:47:26,160 --> 00:47:27,920
Al Auf!

554
00:47:29,720 --> 00:47:33,320
- [ Arabic ]
- [ Al Auf Laughs ]

555
00:47:33,440 --> 00:47:36,680
[ Arabic ]

556
00:47:36,800 --> 00:47:39,360
[ Al Auf Singing ]

557
00:47:39,480 --> 00:47:42,240
[ Katharine ] [song] You'd better be ready
about half past 8:00 [song]

558
00:47:42,360 --> 00:47:44,440
[song] Remember, honey
Don't be late [song]

559
00:47:44,600 --> 00:47:48,120
[song] 'Cause I'll be there
when the band starts playing [song]

560
00:47:48,280 --> 00:47:50,120
[song] Gonna dance off
both my shoes [song]

561
00:47:50,280 --> 00:47:52,480
[song] When they play
the jelly roll blues [song]

562
00:47:52,600 --> 00:47:57,120
[song] Tomorrow night at the
dark-town strutter's ball Music

563
00:47:57,240 --> 00:48:00,320
[ Al Auf Singing ]

564
00:48:00,440 --> 00:48:04,360
[ Chanting In Arabic ]

565
00:48:06,200 --> 00:48:09,080
[ Chanting Continues ]

566
00:48:09,240 --> 00:48:11,640
Give me your hand.

567
00:48:13,560 --> 00:48:15,320
Thanks.

568
00:48:15,480 --> 00:48:19,560
[ Chanting Continues ]

569
00:48:23,240 --> 00:48:26,440
[ Chanting Response ]

570
00:48:30,520 --> 00:48:33,440
[ Chanting Continues ]

571
00:49:47,560 --> 00:49:50,120
Madox!

572
00:49:50,280 --> 00:49:52,160
Madox!

573
00:49:52,280 --> 00:49:54,080
Madox!

574
00:49:54,200 --> 00:49:57,360
- Come quickly! I've found something!
- What is it?

575
00:49:57,520 --> 00:50:01,200
D'Ag! Bermann!
Quick!

576
00:50:17,840 --> 00:50:19,680
My God.

577
00:50:21,400 --> 00:50:23,480
They're swimming.

578
00:50:28,400 --> 00:50:30,640
[ Madox ]
They're swimming.

579
00:50:44,640 --> 00:50:47,880
[ Man Talking: lndistinct ]

580
00:50:49,520 --> 00:50:53,480
[ Men Speaking Arabic ]

581
00:50:58,280 --> 00:51:00,200
Ow!

582
00:51:03,000 --> 00:51:06,120
- You okay?
- Yeah.

583
00:51:25,600 --> 00:51:26,600
[ Giggling ]

584
00:51:30,600 --> 00:51:32,600
How do you explain...

585
00:51:32,760 --> 00:51:35,840
to someone who has
never been here,

586
00:51:35,960 --> 00:51:40,240
feelings which seem
quite normal?

587
00:51:43,280 --> 00:51:46,400
I don't know,
my friend.

588
00:51:46,520 --> 00:51:48,880
I don't know.

589
00:51:52,280 --> 00:51:54,440
- Aaah!
- Kamal!

590
00:51:54,560 --> 00:51:56,520
Bermann,
what are you doing?

591
00:51:56,640 --> 00:51:58,600
Bermann!

592
00:52:00,440 --> 00:52:02,440
Watch out!
Watch out!

593
00:52:04,320 --> 00:52:07,880
[ Men Shouting In Arabic ]

594
00:52:11,920 --> 00:52:15,160
- Get them out of the car!
- Let me help!

595
00:52:15,320 --> 00:52:17,480
Almasy,
are you all right?

596
00:52:17,600 --> 00:52:20,440
Y es, yes,
I'm fine.

597
00:52:22,120 --> 00:52:24,960
My wrist!
Ah!

598
00:52:25,080 --> 00:52:28,680
[ Men Speaking Arabic ]

599
00:52:38,200 --> 00:52:42,720
I'll be back as quick as I can,
36 hours at the outside.

600
00:52:42,840 --> 00:52:44,960
Try to get
an additional radiator.

601
00:52:45,080 --> 00:52:49,720
We can store it between here
and Pottery Hill. And a better jack.

602
00:52:49,880 --> 00:52:53,080
- We planned badly.
- Bermann?

603
00:52:54,400 --> 00:52:57,160
[ Engine Starts ]

604
00:52:57,280 --> 00:52:59,720
[ Arabic ]

605
00:53:06,080 --> 00:53:07,800
Safe journey.

606
00:53:10,520 --> 00:53:12,440
Now what?

607
00:53:12,600 --> 00:53:15,560
[ Madox ] Let's get all
this stuff off.

608
00:53:15,720 --> 00:53:18,520
- I'll stay behind, of course.
- Certainly not.

609
00:53:18,680 --> 00:53:21,400
- I caused the problem. I shall stay.
- You can't. None of you can.

610
00:53:21,560 --> 00:53:24,200
No, I insist. There clearly
isn't room for all of us.

611
00:53:24,320 --> 00:53:27,080
I'm the least able to dig, and I'm not
one of the walking wounded.

612
00:53:27,240 --> 00:53:29,000
It's only one night.

613
00:53:29,160 --> 00:53:32,360
If I remain, it's the most effective
method of persuading my husband...

614
00:53:32,520 --> 00:53:35,720
to abandon whatever he's doing
and come and rescue us.

615
00:53:35,880 --> 00:53:38,280
- All right.
- Come on, let's get going.

616
00:53:40,760 --> 00:53:43,200
[ Engine Starts ]

617
00:54:20,800 --> 00:54:23,960
I thought you might like to
paste them into your book.

618
00:54:30,520 --> 00:54:32,840
Well, we--

619
00:54:32,960 --> 00:54:35,240
We took
photographs.

620
00:54:37,440 --> 00:54:39,560
There's no need.

621
00:54:40,760 --> 00:54:43,160
No, really.
I'd like you to have them.

622
00:54:43,320 --> 00:54:45,600
Well, there's really no need.
This is, um--

623
00:54:45,720 --> 00:54:49,320
This is just a scrapbook.
They are too good.

624
00:54:49,440 --> 00:54:52,920
I should feel obliged.
Thank you.

625
00:55:07,080 --> 00:55:10,040
And that would be unconscionable,
I suppose, wouldn't it?

626
00:55:10,200 --> 00:55:12,560
To feel any obligation.

627
00:55:15,720 --> 00:55:18,440
Yes, of course it would.

628
00:55:36,600 --> 00:55:39,360
[ Men Speaking Arabic ]

629
00:55:46,800 --> 00:55:49,160
You should come
into the shelter.

630
00:55:49,280 --> 00:55:51,120
I'm quite all right,
thank you.

631
00:55:51,280 --> 00:55:54,920
- Look over there.
- What am I looking at?

632
00:55:55,040 --> 00:55:57,240
Do you see what's happening to them,
the stars?

633
00:55:57,400 --> 00:56:00,680
- They're so untidy.
I was trying to rearrange them.
- No, no.

634
00:56:00,800 --> 00:56:02,600
Over there.

635
00:56:06,520 --> 00:56:08,960
In a few minutes,
there'll be no stars.

636
00:56:09,080 --> 00:56:11,280
The air
is filling with sand.

637
00:56:17,560 --> 00:56:20,920
[ Wind Howling ]

638
00:56:29,560 --> 00:56:32,440
[ Shouting ]

639
00:57:04,040 --> 00:57:05,960
[ Panting ]

640
00:57:06,120 --> 00:57:08,760
[ Coughing ]

641
00:57:11,920 --> 00:57:15,320
- This is not very good, is it?
- No.

642
00:57:16,760 --> 00:57:18,520
[ Coughing ]

643
00:57:18,680 --> 00:57:22,120
- We will be all right?
- Y es.

644
00:57:22,280 --> 00:57:24,040
Y es.

645
00:57:25,040 --> 00:57:27,120
Absolutely.

646
00:57:27,280 --> 00:57:30,440
"Y es" is a comfort.
"Absolutely" is not.

647
00:57:46,000 --> 00:57:49,160
Let me tell you
about winds.

648
00:57:49,280 --> 00:57:51,960
There is a, a whirlwind
from southern Morocco,

649
00:57:52,120 --> 00:57:54,200
the Aajej,

650
00:57:54,360 --> 00:57:57,880
against which the Fellahin
defend themselves with knives.

651
00:57:59,640 --> 00:58:03,320
And there is a,
a Ghibli from Tunis.

652
00:58:03,480 --> 00:58:05,640
- A Ghibli?
- A Ghibli.

653
00:58:05,760 --> 00:58:09,320
Which rolls and rolls
and rolls and produces a--

654
00:58:09,440 --> 00:58:12,680
- a rather strange nervous condition.
- [ Laughing ]

655
00:58:14,840 --> 00:58:18,640
And then there is the,
the Harmatten,

656
00:58:18,800 --> 00:58:23,320
a red wind, which mariners called
"a sea of darkness."

657
00:58:24,600 --> 00:58:26,440
And red sand
from this wind...

658
00:58:26,600 --> 00:58:29,320
has flown as far as
the south coast of England,

659
00:58:29,440 --> 00:58:31,720
apparently
producing...

660
00:58:31,840 --> 00:58:35,040
showers so dense that they were
mistaken for blood.

661
00:58:35,160 --> 00:58:37,760
Fiction!

662
00:58:37,880 --> 00:58:41,680
We have a house on that coast
and it has never, never rained blood.

663
00:58:41,840 --> 00:58:44,560
No, it's all true.
Herodotus, your friend.

664
00:58:44,720 --> 00:58:46,800
- My friend.
- Your fri--

665
00:58:46,960 --> 00:58:49,360
He writes about it...

666
00:58:49,520 --> 00:58:52,760
and he writes about...

667
00:58:52,920 --> 00:58:56,080
a, a wind, the Simoon,

668
00:58:56,240 --> 00:58:58,800
which a nation thought
was so evil,

669
00:58:58,880 --> 00:59:02,120
they declared war on it
and marched out against it.

670
00:59:03,480 --> 00:59:05,640
In full battle dress.

671
00:59:07,840 --> 00:59:10,520
Their swords raised.

672
00:59:29,560 --> 00:59:33,800
[ Engine Running,
Horn Honking ]

673
00:59:33,920 --> 00:59:37,640
Katharine, Katharine, I need
to get out your side, quickly.

674
00:59:37,760 --> 00:59:39,960
A car!
Let me out!

675
00:59:40,120 --> 00:59:42,680
Of course.
I'm sorry.

676
00:59:42,840 --> 00:59:44,880
Ridiculous
to fall asleep!

677
00:59:45,040 --> 00:59:46,920
Unforgivable!

678
00:59:47,080 --> 00:59:49,320
Damn!
The flare!

679
00:59:50,720 --> 00:59:52,520
Stop!
Over here!

680
00:59:52,640 --> 00:59:54,720
- [ Honking ]
- Here!

681
00:59:54,840 --> 00:59:57,160
- Stop!
- [ Honking ]

682
00:59:57,320 --> 01:00:00,280
- Madox!
- [ Flare Gun Fires ]

683
01:00:00,440 --> 01:00:03,560
Madox! Madox!

684
01:00:16,600 --> 01:00:18,680
Our tracks
have disappeared.

685
01:00:18,840 --> 01:00:22,080
Madox will calculate how many miles.
He'll soon turn around.

686
01:00:26,920 --> 01:00:29,720
Could I ask you,
please, to...

687
01:00:29,880 --> 01:00:33,080
paste your paintings
into my book.

688
01:00:33,240 --> 01:00:35,240
I-- I should like
to have them.

689
01:00:35,360 --> 01:00:37,640
I should be honoured.

690
01:00:40,280 --> 01:00:43,240
- [ Muffled Honking ]
- The others!

691
01:00:44,440 --> 01:00:46,760
- Oh, God.
- Quick!

692
01:00:46,920 --> 01:00:49,400
[ Honking Continues ]

693
01:00:50,800 --> 01:00:54,400
Oh, how awful!
Are we going to get them out?

694
01:00:54,520 --> 01:00:57,080
Quick!
Get the other shovel!

695
01:00:57,240 --> 01:01:00,040
[ Honking Continues ]

696
01:01:00,160 --> 01:01:03,400
Am I a terrible coward
to ask how much water we have left?

697
01:01:03,520 --> 01:01:07,240
A little in our can. We have, uh, water
in the radiator, which can be drunk.

698
01:01:07,400 --> 01:01:11,360
And, uh, that's not cowardly at all.
It's extremely practical.

699
01:01:11,480 --> 01:01:14,680
We, uh-- Oh, come on!
Come on!

700
01:01:14,840 --> 01:01:18,600
- [ Honking Continues ]
- There's also a plant.

701
01:01:18,720 --> 01:01:22,840
I've never seen it,
but I believe you can cut a piece
the size of a heart from this plant,

702
01:01:23,000 --> 01:01:26,200
and the next morning it'll be filled
with a delicious liquid.

703
01:01:26,360 --> 01:01:29,960
Find that plant,
cut out its heart.

704
01:01:30,080 --> 01:01:33,640
[ Honking Continues ]

705
01:01:33,800 --> 01:01:36,520
Here.

706
01:01:39,120 --> 01:01:41,600
[ Men Shouting ]

707
01:01:41,720 --> 01:01:43,960
Here's the window.

708
01:01:44,120 --> 01:01:46,840
Clear the glass. I'll clear the door.
Mind your hands.

709
01:01:47,000 --> 01:01:49,840
- [ Shouting ]
- It's okay. We're here.

710
01:01:50,000 --> 01:01:52,360
Come on,
clear it quickly.

711
01:01:52,520 --> 01:01:54,280
[ Shouting ]

712
01:01:54,440 --> 01:01:57,240
Hang on.
Coming.

713
01:02:01,360 --> 01:02:05,160
[ Shouting In Arabic ]

714
01:02:05,320 --> 01:02:09,200
- I've got it. I've got it.
- Help them out. Here.

715
01:02:27,120 --> 01:02:29,480
[ Arabic ]

716
01:03:16,600 --> 01:03:18,800
Katharine.

717
01:03:23,520 --> 01:03:25,320
Water.

718
01:03:31,360 --> 01:03:34,480
[ Flare Fires, Explodes ]

719
01:03:40,960 --> 01:03:43,080
[ Katharine ]
Geoffrey's not in Cairo.

720
01:03:46,440 --> 01:03:49,080
He's not actually
a buffoon.

721
01:03:49,240 --> 01:03:51,080
The plane wasn't
a wedding present.

722
01:03:51,200 --> 01:03:53,760
It belongs to the
British government.

723
01:03:53,880 --> 01:03:57,000
They want aerial maps
of the whole of North Africa.

724
01:03:57,160 --> 01:03:59,720
So I think
he's in Ethiopia.

725
01:04:01,800 --> 01:04:04,800
In case you're counting
on his sudden appearance.

726
01:04:08,760 --> 01:04:11,200
And the marriage.

727
01:04:11,320 --> 01:04:13,440
Is that a fiction?

728
01:04:15,880 --> 01:04:18,280
No, the marriage
isn't a fiction.

729
01:04:20,640 --> 01:04:23,040
[ Men Shouting In Arabic ]

730
01:04:23,160 --> 01:04:26,040
- Oh, thank God.
- [ Shouting Continues ]

731
01:04:26,200 --> 01:04:28,040
Thank God.

732
01:04:28,200 --> 01:04:31,080
Madox.
It's Madox.

733
01:04:31,200 --> 01:04:34,320
[ Shouting ]

734
01:04:36,120 --> 01:04:38,800
Am I K. in your book?

735
01:04:38,960 --> 01:04:41,040
I think I must be.

736
01:05:02,960 --> 01:05:04,720
[ Man ]
Chapter one.

737
01:05:04,880 --> 01:05:09,240
"He sat in defiance of municipal orders
astride the gun Zamzammah--"

738
01:05:09,400 --> 01:05:12,760
I can't read these words. I can't
read them. They stick in my throat.

739
01:05:12,920 --> 01:05:15,080
[ Almasy ] Because you're
reading it too fast.

740
01:05:15,240 --> 01:05:17,880
- Not at all.
- You have to read Kipling slowly.

741
01:05:18,040 --> 01:05:21,600
The eye is too impatient.
Think about the speed of his pen.

742
01:05:21,720 --> 01:05:24,600
What is it?
"He sat, comma,

743
01:05:24,760 --> 01:05:27,720
"in defiance of
municipal orders, comma,

744
01:05:27,840 --> 01:05:30,600
"astride the gun
Zamzammah...

745
01:05:30,760 --> 01:05:34,280
on her brick--"
What is it?

746
01:05:34,440 --> 01:05:37,080
"Brick platform, opposite the old
Ajaib-Gher."

747
01:05:37,240 --> 01:05:41,800
"The wonder house, comma,

748
01:05:41,920 --> 01:05:45,320
as the natives called
the Lahore Museum."

749
01:05:45,440 --> 01:05:48,520
It's still there, the cannon,
outside the museum.

750
01:05:48,640 --> 01:05:52,200
Made of metal cups and bowls taken
from every household in the city as tax,

751
01:05:52,320 --> 01:05:54,440
then melted down.

752
01:05:54,600 --> 01:05:58,440
Then later, they fired
the cannon at my people,

753
01:05:58,560 --> 01:06:02,120
comma,
the natives.

754
01:06:02,240 --> 01:06:04,040
Full stop.

755
01:06:04,160 --> 01:06:05,960
[ Slurping ]

756
01:06:06,080 --> 01:06:07,880
What exactly
is it you object to?

757
01:06:08,000 --> 01:06:10,720
The writer,
or what he's writing about?

758
01:06:10,840 --> 01:06:14,520
What I really
object to, Uncle,

759
01:06:14,680 --> 01:06:16,760
is your finishing
all my condensed milk.

760
01:06:16,920 --> 01:06:19,640
And the message everywhere in your book,
however slowly I read it,

761
01:06:19,800 --> 01:06:22,720
that the best thing for lndia
is to be ruled by the British.

762
01:06:22,840 --> 01:06:26,000
Hana, we have discovered
a shared pleasure, the boy and l.

763
01:06:26,160 --> 01:06:28,520
- Arguing about books?
- Condensed milk.

764
01:06:28,680 --> 01:06:30,760
One of the truly
great inventions.

765
01:06:30,880 --> 01:06:33,600
I'll get
another tin.

766
01:06:33,720 --> 01:06:37,160
I didn't like that book either.
It's all about men.

767
01:06:37,280 --> 01:06:40,520
Too many men.
Just like this house.

768
01:06:40,680 --> 01:06:43,880
You like him, don't you?
Your voice changes.

769
01:06:44,040 --> 01:06:46,280
No, I don't
think it does.

770
01:06:46,440 --> 01:06:48,760
Anyway,
he's indifferent to me.

771
01:06:48,920 --> 01:06:51,840
I don't think
it's indifference.

772
01:06:51,960 --> 01:06:54,280
- Hana was just telling me,
you're indifferent...
- Hey!

773
01:06:54,440 --> 01:06:56,360
to her cooking.

774
01:06:57,880 --> 01:06:59,720
Well, I'm indifferent
to cooking.

775
01:06:59,840 --> 01:07:02,440
Not Hana's cooking
in particular.

776
01:07:02,600 --> 01:07:05,640
Have either of you tried
condensed milk sandwiches?

777
01:07:05,800 --> 01:07:07,560
[ Coughing ]

778
01:07:07,720 --> 01:07:10,440
They're very good
with salt.

779
01:07:10,560 --> 01:07:14,200
[ Horns Honking ]

780
01:07:14,360 --> 01:07:17,000
[ People Chattering ]

781
01:07:17,120 --> 01:07:19,560
[ Honking ]

782
01:07:30,200 --> 01:07:32,320
Welcome back,
Madam.

783
01:07:44,560 --> 01:07:46,400
Will you not come in?

784
01:07:46,520 --> 01:07:48,600
No.

785
01:07:48,760 --> 01:07:51,560
- I should go home.
- Will you please come in?

786
01:07:52,800 --> 01:07:54,600
Mrs Clifton.

787
01:07:57,080 --> 01:07:59,520
Don't.

788
01:07:59,640 --> 01:08:01,480
I believe
you still have my book.

789
01:08:16,920 --> 01:08:18,800
Thank you.

790
01:08:30,040 --> 01:08:32,760
[ Chanting In Distance ]

791
01:08:37,840 --> 01:08:39,760
[ Knocking ]

792
01:08:43,520 --> 01:08:45,320
[ Knocking ]

793
01:08:52,440 --> 01:08:54,360
[ Hinges Creak ]

794
01:09:39,640 --> 01:09:42,360
You still have
sand in your hair.

795
01:10:04,920 --> 01:10:07,640
[song] It wouldn't be
make believe [song]

796
01:10:07,800 --> 01:10:10,960
[song] If you believe in me [song]

797
01:10:11,120 --> 01:10:14,400
[song] Without your love [song]

798
01:10:14,520 --> 01:10:16,720
I'm impressed
you can sew.

799
01:10:16,880 --> 01:10:18,640
Good.

800
01:10:25,520 --> 01:10:27,960
You sew very badly.

801
01:10:28,120 --> 01:10:30,280
Well, you don't
sew at all.

802
01:10:30,400 --> 01:10:32,200
A woman should
never learn to sew,

803
01:10:32,320 --> 01:10:34,760
and if she can,
she shouldn't admit to it.

804
01:10:34,880 --> 01:10:36,840
[ Snickers ]

805
01:10:42,280 --> 01:10:45,120
Close your eyes.

806
01:10:45,240 --> 01:10:48,080
It makes it harder still.

807
01:10:50,840 --> 01:10:52,640
Hmm.

808
01:10:56,040 --> 01:10:58,120
When were you
most happy?

809
01:10:58,280 --> 01:11:00,760
Now.

810
01:11:02,200 --> 01:11:04,280
And when were you
least happy?

811
01:11:06,840 --> 01:11:08,680
Now.

812
01:11:13,480 --> 01:11:16,160
- What do you love?
- What do I love?

813
01:11:16,280 --> 01:11:19,120
- Say "everything."
- Let's see.

814
01:11:19,280 --> 01:11:21,200
- Water.
- Mm-hmm.

815
01:11:21,360 --> 01:11:23,360
[ Water Splashing ]

816
01:11:23,480 --> 01:11:26,040
- Fish in it.
- [ Chuckles ]

817
01:11:26,160 --> 01:11:28,840
And hedgehogs.
I love hedgehogs.

818
01:11:29,000 --> 01:11:30,760
And what else?

819
01:11:30,920 --> 01:11:33,640
Marmite.
I'm addicted.

820
01:11:35,480 --> 01:11:38,520
And baths.
But not with other people.

821
01:11:38,680 --> 01:11:40,760
[ Laughing ]

822
01:11:43,120 --> 01:11:45,000
Islands.

823
01:11:47,120 --> 01:11:49,360
And your handwriting.

824
01:11:51,320 --> 01:11:54,160
- Mmm.
- I could go on all day.

825
01:11:54,320 --> 01:11:56,160
Go on all day.

826
01:12:03,920 --> 01:12:06,080
My husband.

827
01:12:08,000 --> 01:12:10,280
And what do you
hate most?

828
01:12:13,840 --> 01:12:15,920
A lie.

829
01:12:17,240 --> 01:12:20,240
What do you
hate most?

830
01:12:23,440 --> 01:12:25,840
Ownership.

831
01:12:26,000 --> 01:12:27,920
Being owned.

832
01:12:29,120 --> 01:12:31,720
When you leave,
you should forget me.

833
01:13:00,680 --> 01:13:03,960
- [ Hana ] Who is this?
- [ Almasy ] Don't you recognize me?

834
01:13:04,120 --> 01:13:07,560
[ Laughing ]
Is it you? So fat.

835
01:13:07,720 --> 01:13:09,800
[ Chuckles ]

836
01:13:12,600 --> 01:13:14,680
- Hmm.
- That's a Christmas cracker.

837
01:13:14,840 --> 01:13:16,720
Firecracker.

838
01:13:16,880 --> 01:13:19,600
- This isn't your handwriting, is it?
- Yes, it is.

839
01:13:21,200 --> 01:13:23,760
"December 22nd.

840
01:13:23,920 --> 01:13:27,200
"Betrayals in war
are childlike...

841
01:13:27,320 --> 01:13:30,280
"compared with our
betrayals during peace.

842
01:13:30,440 --> 01:13:34,440
"New lovers are nervous
and tender...

843
01:13:35,640 --> 01:13:37,720
"but smash everything.

844
01:13:37,880 --> 01:13:42,640
For the heart
is an organ of fire."

845
01:13:45,280 --> 01:13:48,120
"For the heart
is an organ of fire."

846
01:13:52,440 --> 01:13:55,480
I love that.

847
01:13:55,640 --> 01:13:57,480
I believe that.

848
01:13:58,800 --> 01:14:00,560
K?

849
01:14:01,960 --> 01:14:04,680
- Who is K?
- K...

850
01:14:06,040 --> 01:14:08,280
is for Katharine.

851
01:14:11,320 --> 01:14:14,200
[ People Chattering ]

852
01:14:18,560 --> 01:14:20,520
Merry Christmas!
Merry Christmas!

853
01:14:26,360 --> 01:14:29,320
- Say you're sick.
- What? No.

854
01:14:29,480 --> 01:14:32,280
Say you're feeling faint.
The heat.

855
01:14:32,440 --> 01:14:35,080
- No.
- I can't work.

856
01:14:35,200 --> 01:14:39,240
- I can't sleep.
- Katharine.

857
01:14:39,360 --> 01:14:41,360
Coming.

858
01:14:42,920 --> 01:14:46,040
I can't sleep. I wake up shouting
in the middle of the night.

859
01:14:46,200 --> 01:14:50,280
- Geoffrey thinks it's the
thing in the desert, trauma.
- I can still taste you.

860
01:14:53,760 --> 01:14:56,520
- Fill up this. It's empty.
- Oh.

861
01:14:56,680 --> 01:15:00,280
I try to write,
with your taste in my mouth.

862
01:15:03,520 --> 01:15:06,280
Swoon.

863
01:15:06,400 --> 01:15:08,840
I'll catch you.

864
01:15:11,520 --> 01:15:14,600
- Ho, ho, ho, ho, ho!
- [ Child Y elling ]

865
01:15:14,720 --> 01:15:17,560
[ Santa Shouting ]

866
01:15:17,720 --> 01:15:21,360
[ Santa Laughing ]

867
01:15:24,200 --> 01:15:27,240
- Come along with me now!
- Merry Christmas, everyone!

868
01:15:27,360 --> 01:15:29,200
Merry Christmas!

869
01:15:29,360 --> 01:15:32,120
- Katharine! Oh, my goodness!
- Oh.

870
01:15:32,280 --> 01:15:34,120
[ Woman ]
Fetch a chair.

871
01:15:34,240 --> 01:15:36,040
No, I'm fine.

872
01:15:36,200 --> 01:15:38,280
- It's this heat. It's terrible.
- Is she all right?

873
01:15:38,440 --> 01:15:41,080
- She's quite all right.
- Oh, good.

874
01:15:41,240 --> 01:15:43,800
- Are you pregnant?
- Oh, I don't think so, no.

875
01:15:43,960 --> 01:15:45,800
[ Laughing ]
How romantic.

876
01:15:45,920 --> 01:15:48,080
With Fiona, I fell over
every five minutes.

877
01:15:48,240 --> 01:15:51,120
Ronnie christened me
"Lady Downfall."

878
01:15:51,280 --> 01:15:55,200
Do you know, I think I might go inside
and sit down for a few minutes.

879
01:15:55,320 --> 01:15:58,240
- I'll come with you.
- No, I'll be all right. You stay here.

880
01:15:58,360 --> 01:16:00,520
Are you sure?

881
01:16:40,080 --> 01:16:44,360
Music [ Bagpipes ]

882
01:16:44,520 --> 01:16:48,680
[song] Silent night [song]

883
01:16:48,840 --> 01:16:52,880
[song] Holy night [song]

884
01:16:53,040 --> 01:16:56,680
[song] All is calm [song]

885
01:16:56,840 --> 01:17:01,280
[song] All is bright [song]

886
01:17:01,400 --> 01:17:05,800
[song] Round yon virgin [song]

887
01:17:05,920 --> 01:17:08,680
[song] And her child [song]

888
01:17:21,560 --> 01:17:26,560
[song] Silent night [song]

889
01:17:26,680 --> 01:17:30,440
[song] Holy night [song]

890
01:17:30,600 --> 01:17:35,560
[song] All is calm [song]

891
01:17:35,680 --> 01:17:39,480
[song] All is bright [song]
